%S A400086 4,2,2,6,8,7,4,2,4,0,4,3,9,8,6,0,6,6,1,6,8,4,0,2,3,6,3,7,5,1,6,8,1,7,
%T A400086 1,4,5,1,7,5,5,9,8,9,2,6,8,5,8,2,0,8,6,4,2,7,1,7,3,0,6,2,7,7,8,0,4,2,
%U A400086 9,2,4,5,8,1,9,1,1,7,8,1,3,3,1,9,9,8,3
%N A400086 Decimal expansion of the positive ring height in the ten-charge Thomson configuration on the unit sphere.
%C A400086 The configuration is a gyroelongated square dipyramid with one vertex at each pole and two square rings at heights h and -h, rotated by Pi/4 relative to each other. Graham reports its Coulomb-energy optimality in the linked preprint.
%H A400086 Peter Graham, <a href="https://hexagonmath.org/2611.00836v1">The Thomson problem for N = 10</a>, 2026.
%H A400086 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/GyroelongatedSquareDipyramid.html">Gyroelongated Square Dipyramid</a>.
%H A400086 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ThomsonProblem.html">Thomson Problem</a>.
%F A400086 h is the unique stationary point in [0.42,0.43] of the Coulomb energy of this configuration as a function of h (Graham, Section 2.1).
%t A400086 Module[{x, e, h}, e = (4 Sqrt[2] + 2)/Sqrt[1 - x^2] + Sum[8/Sqrt[2 + 2 s x] + 8/Sqrt[2 + 2 x^2 + s Sqrt[2] (1 - x^2)], {s, {-1, 1}}];
%t A400086 	h = x /. FindRoot[Evaluate[D[e, x] == 0], {x, 423/1000}, WorkingPrecision -> 120, PrecisionGoal -> 100];
%t A400086 	First[RealDigits[h, 10, 87]]] (* _Eric W. Weisstein_, Nov 24 2026 *)
%Y A400086 Cf. A400087 (volume of the convex hull).
%K A400086 nonn,cons,new
%O A400086 0,1
%A A400086 _Eric W. Weisstein_, Nov 24 2026
